  "content": {
    "id": "p52-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "$f(x)=\\dfrac{x^2+k}{x^2-k}$. Find the value of $k$ if $f'(-3)=1$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$k=3$ or $27$.",
      "worked": "$u=x^2+k$, $u'=2x$, $v=x^2-k$, $v'=2x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=\\frac{2x(x^2-k)-2x(x^2+k)}{(x^2-k)^2}\\\\f'(x)&=\\frac{-4kx}{(x^2-k)^2}\\\\f'(-3)&=\\frac{12k}{(9-k)^2}\\end{align*}$$\nSince $f'(-3)=1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{12k}{(9-k)^2}&=1\\\\12k&=(9-k)^2\\\\k^2-30k+81&=0\\\\(k-3)(k-27)&=0\\\\k&=3\\text{ or }27.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth values have $9-k\\ne0$, so both are valid.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 58
  },
